is job  and it goes all the way back to the 40s  and 50s when women were actually  programming computers for the military  and for NASA and then in the 60s and 70s  when the industry started to explode  they started doing these personality  tests to identify good programmers and  they decided that good programmers don't  like people  well they denied it yes the industry  decided  you look for people who don't like  people he'll hire far more men than  women and furthermore there is no  evidence to show the people who don't  like people are better at this job but  these tests became widely influential  and they were used for decades and it  has perpetuated this stereotype of the  antisocial mostly white male nerd that  many people imagine when they think  about computers and starting companies  like Facebook they're looking for people  who are casting avatars or these exactly  and think about all the people who don't  look like Mark Zuckerberg who could have  started the next Facebook I think about  all the women who didn't get a chance  and all of the companies that didn't get  a chance because they didn't look the  part you wrote about somebody who liked  to interview people in hot tubs you also  write about drug-fueled sex parties  being part of the culture here how does  that contribute to this you know the Bay  Area has long been a place where you  know people are exploring sexual freedom  and I've talked to both men and women  who are part of the scene there there  are sort of a wide spectrum of behavior  and in a lot of ways it's a lot less  about sex and a lot more about power and  the power dynamic is completely lopsided  you know the women in particular these  are women who are as I said are already  facing several challenges when it comes  to raising money if they go to these  parties they feel like they're  discredited they're not gonna get  funding if they don't go they feel like  they're missing out on an opportunity  because the people holding these parties  are incredibly powerful emily has a  Nietzsche movement come to Silicon  Valley the me2 movement in my view  actually started long before the me2  movement in Hollywood you we have an  investor here her name is Ellen Pao who  sued her venture capital firm in 2012  she lost in 2015 but she sort of won in  the court of public opinion and that  opened the door for more women to come  forward and it didn't happen right away  we saw a Susan Fowler the engineer at  uber who was propositioned for sex by  her manager on her first day on the job  and when she brought it to HR they said  we're gonna let that slide because he's  a high performer and subsequently many  more women have come forward it doesn't  get as much attention because it's not  you know Reese Witherspoon right or Rose  McGowan but it's happening to women in  Silicon Valley every day well it is an  incredibly well timed book Emily for the  moment in American reconciliation as it  were Emily Chang with growtopia thanks  so much
 

